{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1451512-v20-potence-vicissitude-combo-in-a-way-i-hadn-t-noticed","id":1451512,"title":"V20 - Potence + Vicissitude combo in a way I hadn't noticed!","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-06-09T17:07:40-07:00","id":1451512,"title":"V20 - Potence + Vicissitude combo in a way I hadn't noticed!","content":"Spend 1 blood to activate Potence for the turn. Strength checks now get automatic successes equal to your Potence.<br /> <br /> Bonecraft an enemy. Strength + Medicine check.<br /> <br /> Assuming you are Generation 9 or lower, you can activate both powers and greatly increase your odds of scoring 5 or more successes. If that works the target loses half of their blood pool instantly as their ribs pierce their heart.<br /> <br /> Against a typical enemy this is a waste of time since with good Potence chances are you could just take them out with some heavy hits. But against a &quot;boss&quot; enemy like a recently risen Methuselah, cutting their blood down by half could greatly weaken them.<br /> <br /> Just a combo I'd never considered before, somewhat relevant to Tzimisce with Potence and Blood Brothers with Vicissitude for instance.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-11T04:38:35-07:00","id":1451634,"title":"","content":"An implicit rule I've seen most games run with is that disciplines operate based on your raw, unmodified stats - Auspex doesn't affect Perception checks of other disciplines, Enchanting Voice doesn't affect Dominate, Blood Buff doesn't affect the activation checks, etc.<br /> <br /> Ignoring that - sure, nice I guess. I still wouldn't bother with bonecrafting a spike through their heart though. Just use the first application of Bonecraft, and with five successes you've given the 5 levels of lethal damage. That's way better than doing no damage but wasting their blood supply.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12892-kammerer","name":"Kammerer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12892_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-11T04:51:01-07:00","id":1451637,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kammerer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1451512-v20-potence-vicissitude-combo-in-a-way-i-hadn-t-noticed?p=1451634#post1451634\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">An implicit rule I've seen most games run with is that disciplines operate based on your raw, unmodified stats - Auspex doesn't affect Perception checks of other disciplines, Enchanting Voice doesn't affect Dominate, Blood Buff doesn't affect the activation checks, etc.<br /> <br /> Ignoring that - sure, nice I guess. I still wouldn't bother with bonecrafting a spike through their heart though. Just use the first application of Bonecraft, and with five successes you've given the 5 levels of lethal damage. That's way better than doing no damage but wasting their blood supply.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's very fair - torpor is better than wasting blood, and more reliable.<br /> <br /> I guess wasting blood is the best application vs an enemy with absurdly high levels of health or soak.<br /> <br /> EDIT: taking a second look, just using Celerity to grapple, bite and bite is probably better overall. Activating the potence, or keeping it passive, will produce a lot of damage. And Aggravated damage, unlike the bonecrafting, can't be soaked by most. The bone move is good for Vampires you want kept alive of course.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 06-11-2021, 08:14 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-12T03:16:30-07:00","id":1451824,"title":"","content":"I'll argue that you can't soak damage from Bonecraft. Going by p.272-273, Soak subtracts from damage <i>dice.</i> Bonecraft applies damage <i>levels.</i> Which are the aftermath of soaking, and then rolling damage <i>dice.</i> So by structure of an attack roll, Bonecraft skips straight to the end of &quot;Step 3: Resolution&quot; with the Bonecraft replacing every other step.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12892-kammerer","name":"Kammerer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12892_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-12T11:27:18-07:00","id":1451883,"title":"","content":"Vicissitude is already very powerful and I usually run with the interpretation if they don't say it's unsoakable, you can soak it. This makes a lot of Banu Haqim players mad at me (I'm sure you know what power I'm referring to), but let's be reasonable. Fortitude is already kind of trash in Revised/V20, why then turn around and give abilities the power to ignore soak when you don't have to? And yes, if you want a REAL nasty Potence/Vicissitude combo, read Horrid Form. As I recall there's a line in there about how you can use Strength to Intimidate people. I'm sure you can see how that can be broken in half by Dread Gaze or Command...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24241-lynceus","name":"Lynceus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24241_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-12T14:05:44-07:00","id":1451905,"title":"","content":"There's a few reasons this ain't so great. <br /> 1- You can't vicissitude with a weapon. Potence is strong, Potence+weapon is stronger. <br /> 2- You can't celerity and Vicissitude. <br /> 3- I reckon you need good contact to vicissitude someone, and while i'd let you do it through a thin shirt, most people are going to be wearing thick coats at night. Deliberately aiming for exposed skin is not great. <br /> 4-blood expenditure. <br /> 5-masquerade (Yes, the Sabbat do care, they're just pretending not to)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-12T23:26:44-07:00","id":1451952,"title":"","content":"Wait, why can't you use Celerity with Vicissitude? The extra actions granted by Celerity must be Physical- I would think a Strength + Body Crafts roll would totally count.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24241-lynceus","name":"Lynceus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24241_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-13T11:17:49-07:00","id":1452019,"title":"","content":"My mistake. You are correct (at least technically, I'm not sure about RAI) Also I'm now wondering if the Grapple you need to do as part of the offensive Vicissitude does it's own damage before you get to the bone bending; The potence would help with that. <br /> Still, it's rather blood and XP intensive. <br /> <br /> There are no rules for armour getting in the way of Vicissitude. However in the vicissitude introduction it does state &quot;The wielder must establish skin-to-skin contact and<br /> must often manually sculpt the desired result for these<br /> powers to take effect&quot; <br /> <br /> I feel iffy about using potence auto-successes for precise grappling. Perhaps we'd need to do a -You need at least this many successes on grapple someone in this much clothing- to vicissitude table.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-13T12:32:05-07:00","id":1452032,"title":"","content":"Potence is sometimes an issue because the Discipline is, unusually for a Storyteller System game, VERY clear about what it does, adding to all Strength rolls. And then the game gives us some things that seem very 'sus' when used with Potence. Clinch? Tackle? Bonecraft? Horrid Form? Esprit de Corps*? Did the developers forget Potence was a thing when they wrote the rules (except in V20 when they got around to writing Obtenebration 3...)? It seems bizarre that they could, but like most things with the game, it's up to what your Troupe decides to go with. On the one hand, since Potence is a fairly limited Discipline outside of combat or the occasional Physical challenge, allowing it to have these interactions is usually fine (even using Potence to double dip on a Tackle isn't usually that bad, because Bashing damage is pathetic...not to mention I think I've seen an actual knockdown result like once ever...) but you are correct in that, for the most part, using Vicissitude in combat is generally not worth it, even if you're playing some Bratovitch Tzimisce with Potence. Unless you're playing in a group that thinks using Bonecraft for an &quot;insta-kill&quot; is kosher, I suppose (sorry folks, I think Health levels exist for a reason!).<br /> <br /> *Esprit de Corps is the goofiest example I know of. The power literally REQUIRES Potence 5, yet has a Strength roll and that's never addressed in it's system. Then again, who even read the Libellus Sanguinus books?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24241-lynceus","name":"Lynceus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24241_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-13T12:58:12-07:00","id":1452039,"title":"","content":"Clinch and tackle make perfect sense with potence. If anything, the weird rule is that you use dex rather than strength for bite, when you do so from a grapple; being dextrous won't let you pull someone in for a bite, being strong will. Potence should also benefit your running speed but it doesn't because i think the writers were taking cues from comic books...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-13T13:57:51-07:00","id":1452054,"title":"","content":"The house rule limiting the number of disciplines the player can activate per turn to one also does a lot to bring the otherwise overpowered Banu Haqim back down to Earth, instead of letting them rack up instakills by combining Celerity with the assassination discipline from whichever edition you're playing.<br /> <br /> Combining disciplines is what devotions are for, in my opinion.<br /> <br /> As an aside, I can imagine there being a bit of an arms race during the Anarch Revolt - with young Anarchs using Bonecraft as a equalizer against Tzimisce elders, and the Acid Blood power gaining popularity among the elders who survived as a retributive defense.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11599-reasor","name":"Reasor","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-13T23:16:18-07:00","id":1452117,"title":"","content":"A little off-topic: I never saw the Banu Haqim as overpowered; heck, I'd take a City Gangrel over one of them any night. But really, Obfuscate is the really broken Discipline there. The scariest killing machine I ever saw in a game was a perfectly ordinary Nosferatu jumping people out of nowhere and pulling them into a nigh-inescapable death hug and biting them to death.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24241-lynceus","name":"Lynceus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24241_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-14T00:46:10-07:00","id":1452127,"title":"","content":"Actually, Quietus is garbage in all the editions I've read. (Which is why I tried to &quot;fix&quot; it) <br /> 1 is great, unless you're playing DAV20, in which case it's the worst first dot imaginable. <br /> 2 is good <br /> 3 is terrible against vampires because they can heal it rather easily and are often quite resistive in the first place. Against mortals you are better off just using regular poison or some other method. It also leaves no room for doubt that an assamite made the kill. it's a willpower drain <br /> 4 offers you what other disciplines are doing at 2. <br /> 5 is just 4 but ranged. In DAV20 it's actually pretty good in a way i don't think is fitting/balanced<br /> <br /> Also the vizier path in DAV20 is broken-powerful.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-14T08:37:55-07:00","id":1452170,"title":"","content":"Just a suggestion here, but I house rule combat applications of Vicissitude thus:<br /> <br /> Starting at level 2, characters with Vicissitude can make attacks with it that deal lethal damage (even at level 2 you can do something like rip a muscle or organ out, or punch a hole through their torso). It resolves as an unarmed attack, but the character substitutes their Body Crafts rating for weapon damage. These attacks <i>can</i> be healed with blood expenditure regardless of generation limits unlike other, more deliberate, usage of Vicissitude as a balancing factor.<br /> <br /> At Vicissitude 3, the &quot;blood pool halved&quot; effect is just a bonus effect for having dealt five lethal post-soak.<br /> <br /> Is this nasty with Potence? Well...yeah. So is everything else in the realm of melee combat (and thrown weapons). That's kind of Potence's thing, it's just part of the game.<br /> <br /> Bit of a post-script on retrospection: Vicissitude is an open-ended, broad, and inconsistent enough discipline ST's really have to get their hands dirty at times to get what they want out of it, thematically and mechanically. And, a lot of its power derives from how much freedom ST's grant PC's and NPC's to get the most out of it. In the big scheme of things, it's really not <i>that</i> far off in power and application from other disciplines or combinations thereof (ask me how my favorite discipline combo in the game is Obtenebration and Melpominee), but it takes a lot of finesse on a ST's part to get it from its baseline to where they really want it to be.<br /> <br /> Contrary to a lot of opinions, that's not necessarily a <i>bad</i> thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":431,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","user":"Theodrim","date":". 06-14-2021, 12:20 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-14T11:33:58-07:00","id":1452189,"title":"","content":"Vicissitude is one of those &quot;Mother May I?&quot; powers that really gets obnoxious quick as you try to keep up with player creativity. When used by NPC's, it can have as much power as you want it to have (&quot;Oh well you see, he's reinforced his bones with metal and has two layers of graphite underneath his skin&quot;). And that open-endedness is all upside. But when a player gets it, you have to suddenly figure out hard limits that the power doesn't really give. You could state that it does what it says it does, nothing more, but there are a host of NPC Tzimisce who have done all kinds of wild things in the setting, so that breaks immersion if you can't give yourself extra arms or create super ghouls. Sometimes this can be explained away by Devotions or &quot;Elder Powerz&quot; but not always. So in a way, it's kind of like having a Sphere of Magic in Mage, and you have to work out all the guidelines yourself. A big book of Vicissitude Do's and Don'ts would have been nice at some point. Allowing it to be vicious is fine, most Disciplines can be, in the right circumstances (sorry, Fortitude, you're still bad). But as always, Potence and Celerity become an issue when they become a force multiplier for a given Discipline, much how I'm sure many first-time Storytellers freak out when Potence is combined with Protean for the first time &quot;What do you mean, you just did 8 levels of aggravated to the Sheriff?!&quot;. Blood Sorcery, Dominate, Presence, Dementation, and the like don't (usually) get that kind of benefit, forcing you to be extra careful. I've run Vicissitude with fairly few limits on what it can do, and the results have been fairly amazing, quickly turning the Chronicle into a superhero game. Which was fine at the time, but in my current Chronicle, I've massively weakened the Tzimisce (and those pesky Lasombra) to keep things more 'street level'. So yes, by the rules, Potence (and Celerity) can make Vicissitude more dangerous. Whether or not you're alright with that is a decision the Storyteller has to make.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24241-lynceus","name":"Lynceus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24241_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-15T02:48:40-07:00","id":1452297,"title":"","content":"The simple Fortitude buff:<br /> <br /> Each rank of Fortitude a Vampire has grants them one additional Bruised health level.<br /> <br /> That way the power of Fortitude grants some consistency, even if your Soak dice don't work out or you're hit with unsoakable effects. It ensures that Fortitude remains a good investment at 3 and beyond, instead of suffering from a horrible case of big investment low reward. Who wants to buy Fortitude 5, really? An extra health level at least sweetens the deal a tad.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-15T03:01:41-07:00","id":1452298,"title":"","content":"The simple fortitude buff is to use pre-revised rules, where you could soak aggravated with stamina unless it was fire, sunlight or faith, and things were balanced around that. <br /> <br /> I also add that Fortitude adds to dice to prevent you from being knocked over, and also makes you immune to electricity if your fortitude exceeds the damage it can do. I run with the idea that Fortitude doesn't make you tougher in a conventional sense, it negates forces against you. <br /> and also you can spend blood to turn die into levels, as with Potence.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-15T03:59:32-07:00","id":1452303,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1451512-v20-potence-vicissitude-combo-in-a-way-i-hadn-t-noticed?p=1452298#post1452298\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The simple fortitude buff is to use pre-revised rules, where you could soak aggravated with stamina unless it was fire, sunlight or faith, and things were balanced around that.<br /> <br /> I also add that Fortitude adds to dice to prevent you from being knocked over, and also makes you immune to electricity if your fortitude exceeds the damage it can do. I run with the idea that Fortitude doesn't make you tougher in a conventional sense, it negates forces against you.<br /> and also you can spend blood to turn die into levels, as with Potence.</div> </div> </div> </div>Huh. Why did they change that? It seems weird to give the Gangrel and Setites an easy means of damage which 90% of vampires can't soak at all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-15T06:25:42-07:00","id":1452317,"title":"","content":"We're getting tangential, but Fortitude in and by itself isn't the root cause of its own problem. Fortitude is fine, especially post-V20 where blood points can be spent to convert Fortitude dots into auto-soak, and armor rating was finally normalized to other WoD games to apply to (non-bane) aggravated.<br /> <br /> The root cause of the problem is combat overage. I've run without combat overage at my tables for over 15 years, and that one change fixed practically the entire combat system on its own. Action economy loses its premium and players can't brute force combat with huge dice pools and Celerity, players actually have to invest in Strength/Potence if they want characters to hit hard, thrown weapons are <i>damn</i> good by comparison, players actually use combat maneuvers (that aren't clench/grapple) for once, and players get to think laterally during combat to jockey for situational advantages rather than just declare attacks and defenses.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":431,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","user":"Theodrim","date":". 06-15-2021, 09:31 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-15T07:20:04-07:00","id":1452321,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1451512-v20-potence-vicissitude-combo-in-a-way-i-hadn-t-noticed?p=1452317#post1452317\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">We're getting tangential, but Fortitude in and by itself isn't the root cause of its own problem. Fortitude is fine, especially post-V20 where blood points can be spent to convert Fortitude dots into auto-soak, and armor rating was finally normalized to other WoD games to apply to (non-bane) aggravated.<br /> <br /> The root cause of the problem is combat overage. I've run without combat overage at my tables for over 15 years, and that one change fixed practically the entire combat system on its own. Action economy loses its premium and players can't brute force combat with huge dice pools and Celerity, players actually have to invest in Strength/Potence if they want characters to hit hard, thrown weapons are <i>damn</i> good by comparison, players actually use combat maneuvers (that aren't clench/grapple) for once, and players get to think laterally during combat to jockey for situational advantages rather than just declare attacks and defenses.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> When did this change to Fortitude occur? Is that a DAV20 thing or a 5th edition thing? Because I know as of V20, Fortitude works just like Revised, with no option to spend blood to increase it's effect.<br /> <br /> As far as overage (which I always called &quot;overkill&quot;), getting rid of it doesn't really solve the problem of Fortitude by itself. Fortitude has no active effect. It grants no utility, it doesn't help you win combat or other dangerous situations, it simply helps you &quot;not lose&quot;. And it's not even really good at that. A few extra dice are effectively meaningless, since the rule of 1 is designed to make more dice matter less as you achieve larger die pools. A Specialty can overcome this, but in the case of Fortitude, that requires expending resources in a trait other than Fortitude (ie, Stamina of 4 or greater), which makes Stamina a far better investment than this Discipline (and that's IF your Storyteller allows a &quot;soak damage&quot; Specialty- I've encountered some who think the idea is rather cheap, like taking a Strength specialty that applies to &quot;damage&quot;, but I digress). This is then countered not just by &quot;overage&quot;, but the fact that Potence completely counters it's effect- and Potence offers other options besides raw damage. Potence users can apply almost inescapable grapples, jump higher, and try to smash you with very large objects that can inflict even more damage.<br /> <br /> The fact that any weapon gives you extra damage dice alone is enough to make Fortitude a bit questionable. If Bob the Brujah has Str 5 and Potence 5, and picks up a fire axe that does, say Str +3 Lethal, the guy with Stam 5 and Fortitude 5 is already at a disadvantage, unless he's wearing armor of some kind (which at level 3, lowers his Dexterity by 1). And the fact that Fortitude doesn't resist anything but damage- there are a host of Disciplines, even those that can affect your body (such as Vicissitude) that don't care about your Fortitude.<br /> <br /> Then you bring in aggravated damage, and while it's nice that you're allowed to soak SOMETHING, 5 dice tops against some Brujah's Str +1 bite is a very shallow advantage. Now this is all by design- Vampire is a terrible game for combat, and combat is DEADLY and a great risk for someone's immortality. You're supposed to avoid it. But the fact that Fortitude is one of the more common Disciplines, and it's really bad at it's job quickly makes players avoid bothering with high levels of it.<br /> <br /> You're better off investing in 8th Generation and using blood buff to Stamina if you want soak (and even then, you're probably better off with Dexterity in most situations). I've toyed with all kinds of variations of Fortitude; in my current chronicle, odd levels add extra Bruised Health levels and even levels lower soak difficulties by 1, and I allow Stamina to soak aggravated, and it still can't help my Gangrel player avoid damage in combat the way the Brujah player can by simply having an extra action or two to dodge attacks.<br /> <br /> Even if Fortitude automatically soaked damage, it's still worse than Potence. It would have to apply to things other than just raw damage to have a shot at being useful, because again, it has no other benefit than defense. And the sad part is, I want Fortitude to be good, but I know it's not.<br /> <br /> Oh something I just thought of- if overage isn't a thing, then wouldn't it be more likely that players could make targeted attacks against unarmored locations more viable? Food for thought.<br /> <br /> -11twiggins, the reason they changed it was they wanted aggravated damage to be scary. They wanted vampires to fear the sun and fire. Granted, the higher soak difficulties exposure can bring does a lot to do this, but I'm sure at some point, the developers saw some Gangrel ignore a burning building with 7-8 dice of soak and were like &quot;no that's not right&quot;. This is actually an important advantage to the Gangrel, as I found out by allowing better soak in my game. Gangrel are supposed to be dangerous, but the low damage of claws, and their lack of Potence really hurts in a game where it's not an attack against a limited defense. In most games, a starting Gangrel can rip apart foes and that makes people fear them just a bit more.*<br /> <br /> The fact that non-Gangrel (or Followers/Ministry) can rather cheaply get this same benefit is well known (to the point that the LARP community simply says &quot;Gangrel do not teach Fortitude&quot;, a lie that has crept into a lot of tabletop groups. Go read a few 'By Night' books, and you'll see tons of non-Gangrel with Protean). I've solved this by switching Protean levels 2 and 3 (something I think Requiem did), and it's worked out great.<br /> <br /> *What I ended up doing was allowing my Gangrel players easy access to a Devotion (Talons of Fenris) to give them a few extra damage dice and an armor rending effect. And using the Revised Gangrel Clanbook rules for stat buffs for their animal forms.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24241-lynceus","name":"Lynceus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24241_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-15T08:40:37-07:00","id":1452335,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lynceus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1451512-v20-potence-vicissitude-combo-in-a-way-i-hadn-t-noticed?p=1452321#post1452321\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> When did this change to Fortitude occur? Is that a DAV20 thing or a 5th edition thing? Because I know as of V20, Fortitude works just like Revised, with no option to spend blood to increase it's effect...Fortitude has no active effect. It grants no utility, it doesn't help you win combat or other dangerous situations, it simply helps you &quot;not lose&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>DAV20. Adding Fortitude dice to Stamina-related dice pools (as Potence does Strength), and spending blood for auto-soak, were already common house rules to normalize Fortitude, and OPP finally just published them as part of the package as-written. It actually makes Fortitude quite appealing for the smattering of discipline powers that use Stamina.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...A few extra dice are effectively meaningless, since the rule of 1 is designed to make more dice matter less as you achieve larger die pools.... </div> </div> </div>That isn't the case at all. Any given die has a greater likelihood of resulting in a success than a 1, except in the case of difficulty 10 rolls in which a success and a 1 have the same 10% likelihood per die. And given combat rolls with few notable exceptions operate based upon the standard difficulty of 6, each die individually has a 50% chance of resulting in a success whereas it has a 10% chance of a 1. The overall impact of this is far less important than you make it out to be.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...unless he's wearing armor of some kind (which at level 3, lowers his Dexterity by 1. </div> </div> </div>Yes, and this only has an impact on damage on a per-case basis in circumstances in which overage applies. Because with overage, attack dice <i>are</i> damage dice on a 2:1 basis, because excess attack successes convert into damage dice. Which means defense dice, as opposed to soak dice, and the actions spent to make those defense rolls, are at a premium because they <i>also</i> function for all intents and purposes as soak by way of reducing damage pools. This is precisely why attack/defense pools and action economy are king in WoD, and Dexterity double dips.<br /> <br /> When you remove or nerf overage, that double dipping no longer applies and characters can actually wear armor without crippling themselves. Thereby, putting weapons and armor on parity.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But the fact that Fortitude is one of the more common Disciplines, and it's really bad at it's job quickly makes players avoid bothering with high levels of it. </div> </div> </div>I <i>know</i> it's &quot;bad&quot;. I'm pointing out <i>why</i> it's &quot;bad&quot; -- it's ill-designed for the combat system it fits into. And the <i>combat system</i> is, itself, bad. Ask yourself, would the nature of this conversation fundamentally change were we talking exclusively about lethal instead of aggravated?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...and it still can't help my Gangrel player avoid damage in combat the way the Brujah player can by simply having an extra action or two to dodge attacks. </div> </div> </div>Well...yeah. That's what happens when every success on an attack beyond the first, when the target can't defend, converts to damage dice. Damage pools skyrocket, and any level of soak the target may be capable of bringing to bear ceases to matter.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Oh something I just thought of- if overage isn't a thing, then wouldn't it be more likely that players could make targeted attacks against unarmored locations more viable? Food for thought. </div> </div> </div>Oh trust me, after fifteen years' experience running without overage, it absolutely does make called shots matter. The situation <i>with</i> overage is so out of whack, the difficulty penalty on the attack roll makes characters likely to have <i>less</i> damage dice on the resolution, because of how excess attack successes convert to damage. Remove overage from the equation, and those additional damage dice from called shots are a lot more valuable, in addition to the potential of bypassing armor, making the cost-benefit ratio of called shots a lot more enticing.<br /> <br /> That makes combat <i>better</i> overall, since it gives players a bigger toybox that bring tangible benefits to the table.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-15T09:40:52-07:00","id":1452342,"title":"","content":"Okay, getting rid of overage seems to be a good idea!<br /> <br /> So the idea would be that if you roll 10 successes on an attack, that doesn't deal additional damage. It's either hit or miss. That means Celerity is less busted - it helps you hit, but it doesn't help you hit harder. Perhaps a 6th level Celerity power would let you add overage back in, call it Deadly Precision or something...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-15T11:17:03-07:00","id":1452358,"title":"","content":"Getting rid of it completely seems a bit extreme. I reduce the difficulty of targeted attacks (slightly) and set the maximum amount of carry-over to 4.It's like that &quot;five sucesses are exceptional&quot; kinda rule prevailent in the rest of the rules and more successes aren't useful.&quot; This works more on a simulationist level; You can't score 10 damage because you got 11 successes with a BB gun, but a barely-successful sniper round is going to be very different from an excellent shot. It'll make the difference between an instant kill and a horrible wound. <br /> <br /> I also do stuff like 'oh you were aiming for the torso but only got 1 success so you just an arm' and 'You aimed at him and got five successes, you bypassed her armour/got a headshot/caused him to lose his footing. This also means instead of doing shit like &quot;You aim for his eye, that's +3 difficulty, oh you got no successes so you didn't hit him at all&quot; It's more &quot;You aim for his eye, that's +2 difficulty, Oh you got 1 success, you hit his head, but you don't get the eye&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-15T12:16:16-07:00","id":1452362,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1451512-v20-potence-vicissitude-combo-in-a-way-i-hadn-t-noticed?p=1452358#post1452358\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Getting rid of it completely seems a bit extreme...</div> </div> </div> </div>One thing I've been doing in my TC game, is allowing overage at (net successes - 4) to allow characters to get overage starting at the threshold for dramatic success. That's for melee combat, mind; I re-added full overage on ranged attacks, given how anemic they tend to be by default, and how very little adds to ranged damage, let alone in ways that cause damage to increase exponentially. Thus, players get something in exchange for well-executed plans, sneak attacks and undefended attacks (which aren't as big a deal in DAV20 for how multiple actions are resolved), and pushing their dice pools higher than really necessary.<br /> <br /> That way, it's not a massive bonus and not something that's going to monkey wrench an encounter due to a single statistically-unlikely good or bad roll, but it is something.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-16T06:10:16-07:00","id":1452457,"title":"","content":"Maybe it's because I've been using a die roller program for online games for a very long time but large die pools have always shown to be very wonky. The fact that each die has a 1 in 10 chance to remove a success but only (usually) a 5 in 10 chance to succeed has always made large die pools act in bizarre ways. Firearms are a big example of this- I've often seen someone gather a large pool of damage dice through various means (sniping or point blank shots being prime examples, plus the fact that if you have no cover, the only way to dodge is to fall prone) and the results have always been...ludicrous. I recently saw someone roll TWENTY FIVE dice of damage after a successful Ambush and only get 8 damage successes, 5 of which were soaked. So I started to realize that, at some point, adding another die seemed to have a minimal impact on the proceedings. I started to consider this might have been the intent. My solution was to make 10 = 2 universal, and now specialties ignore ones (similar to the effect of Charmed Existence) and we still see bizarre results. If anything, the result of this chance is we've been seeing smaller die pools get more successes than dice rolled, while the larger ones still remain unwieldly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24241-lynceus","name":"Lynceus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24241_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-16T08:39:12-07:00","id":1452484,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lynceus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1451512-v20-potence-vicissitude-combo-in-a-way-i-hadn-t-noticed?p=1452457#post1452457\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Maybe it's because I've been using a die roller program for online games...</div> </div> </div> </div>That's why. Random number generator scripts are only pseudo-random; they're deterministic, because they propagate a string of integers based upon a seed generated at the time the script was run. You can get <i>more random</i> results from scripts that rely on <i>external</i> data for seed generation, but the results are still going to be a string of integers based upon that seed. You <i>can </i>get truly random results from a computer, but I don't have the few dozen million bucks to buy a quantum computer and the infrastructure to support it, to do the same job a $10 set of dice does faster.<br /> <br /> I'm not going to blow smoke and rainbows, dice are deterministic too. They're just a lot less deterministic than a computer program, let alone perceptibly so.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}